              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "A \"G-Wagon\" (officially known as the **Mercedes-Benz G-Class**) is a **luxury mid-size, four-wheel-drive SUV (Sport Utility Vehicle)**. \n\nThe name \"G-Wagon\" is short for ***Gel\u00e4ndewagen***, which is German for \"terrain vehicle\" or \"off-road vehicle.\" \n\nSeveral defining characteristics set the G-Wagon apart from other vehicles on the road:\n\n### 1. Traditional Body-on-Frame Construction\nWhile most modern SUVs are built using \"unibody\" construction (similar to passenger cars), the G-Wagon retains a traditional, heavy-duty **body-on-frame** design (like a pickup truck). This makes it incredibly robust and durable under harsh conditions.\n\n### 2. Iconic Boxy Styling\nThe G-Wagon is famous for its retro, military-inspired boxy shape, flat glass, and external door hinges. It has maintained this distinct look with only minor cosmetic updates since its original introduction in 1979. \n\n### 3. Ultimate Off-Road Capability\nThough it is rarely used for this by its wealthy owners, the G-Wagon was originally developed as a military and utilitarian vehicle. It is one of the few production vehicles equipped with **three fully locking differentials** (front, center, and rear), allowing it to conquer nearly any terrain, steep incline, or deep water.\n\n### 4. A Luxury Status Symbol\nOver the decades, Mercedes-Benz transitioned the G-Wagon from a rugged workhorse into an ultra-luxury status symbol. Today, it features high-end leather interiors, cutting-edge technology, and premium audio systems. \n\n### Common Variations:\n* **Mercedes-AMG G 63:** The highly popular, high-performance version tuned by Mercedes' AMG division, featuring a powerful twin-turbo V8 engine.\n* **Mercedes-Benz G 550 / G 500:** The standard luxury version.\n* **Mercedes-Benz G 580 with EQ Technology:** The fully electric (EV) version of the G-Wagon, powered by four electric motors (one at each wheel).",
